---
/**
 * Home (pillar page, RECETTE §5, §21): the online degree cost calculator on the first screen, then the reference
 * text: what colleges charge per credit by type, five steps to a budget, the twenty largest online schools, the
 * states, completion, what the federal files cannot say, the list of pages and the sources.
 * Every figure comes from the federal files built by scripts/build-data.py or from the engine.
 */
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Tool from './Tool.astro';
import Sources from './Sources.astro';
import Clamp from './Clamp.astro';
import { route } from '../i18n/routes';
import { PAGES } from '../lib/guides';
import { groupLabel, GROUPS_ORDER } from '../i18n/nav';
import { helpers } from '../lib/helpers';
import { AUTHOR_NAME } from '../data/site-config';
import { faqSchema, webApplicationSchema, websiteSchema, organizationSchema } from '../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const h = helpers(true);
const { P, N, D, B, E, m, num, pc } = h;

const bach = E.degreeCost({ perCredit: N.medPub4, credits: P.credits.bachelor, transfer: 0, feesPerYear: N.medFeePub4, creditsPerTerm: 15, termsPerYear: 2, grantsPerYear: 0 });
const shareOnline = N.exc / N.tot;
const types = [
  ['Public university, state resident', m(N.medPub4), m(N.medFeePub4), m(N.medPub4 * 120 + 4 * N.medFeePub4)],
  ['Public university, from another state', m(N.medPub4out), m(N.medFeePub4), m(N.medPub4out * 120 + 4 * N.medFeePub4)],
  ['Private nonprofit university', m(N.medNpf4), m(N.medFeeNpf4), m(N.medNpf4 * 120 + 4 * N.medFeeNpf4)],
  ['Private for-profit university', m(N.medFp4), m(N.medFeeFp4), m(N.medFp4 * 120 + 4 * N.medFeeFp4)],
];
const largest = D.FEATURED.slice().sort((a, b) => (b.exc ?? 0) - (a.exc ?? 0)).slice(0, 12).map((f) => [
  `${B.name(f.n)}<br><span class="text-xs text-navy-600">${f.st}, ${E.SECTOR_LABEL[f.ctrl] ?? ''}</span>`,
  num(f.exc ?? 0),
  f.h2 ? `${m(f.h2)} a credit` : `${m(f.t2 ?? 0)} a year, flat`,
  m(B.schoolCost(f, 120).total),
  f.om8 != null ? pc(f.om8, 0) : 'n/a',
]);
const topStates = Object.values(D.STATES).sort((a, b) => b.exc - a.exc).slice(0, 8);
const figures = [
  { fig: num(N.exc), what: `students studying fully online in ${P.ipeds.enrollmentTerm}, ${pc(shareOnline, 0)} of all college students` },
  { fig: num(N.nOnline), what: 'colleges with at least 50 of them, all eligible for federal student aid' },
  { fig: m(N.medPub4), what: 'median price per credit at a public university, for state residents' },
  { fig: `${num(E.finishers(100, N.medOm8Online))} of 100`, what: 'students at mostly online colleges with a degree or certificate 8 years after starting' },
];
const faqs = [
  { q: 'How much does an online degree cost in total?', a: `At the median public university, a 120-credit bachelor’s comes to about ${m(bach.gross)} for a state resident: ${m(bach.tuition)} of tuition and ${m(bach.fees)} of required fees over four years. The same degree from another state costs about ${m(N.medPub4out * 120 + 4 * N.medFeePub4)}. Credits you already have, grants and your pace change the total; the calculator above counts all three.` },
  { q: 'Is an online degree cheaper than a campus degree?', a: 'Tuition is often the same: most colleges report one price per credit, whatever the format. The savings come from elsewhere: no room and board, no move, and the chance to keep working. Some universities charge online students a separate rate, higher or lower, so compare the program’s own tuition page with the figures here.' },
  { q: 'Where do these prices come from?', a: `From IPEDS, the federal survey every college that takes student aid must answer, for the ${P.ipeds.chargesYear} academic year, and from College Scorecard for award rates, Pell shares, debt and accreditors. Nothing is taken from college rankings or lead-generation sites, and no school pays to appear on this site.` },
  { q: 'Can I use federal student aid for an online degree?', a: `Yes, at any of the ${num(N.n)} colleges counted here: all of them take part in the federal aid programs. The aid itself depends on your FAFSA, the program and how many credits you take each term. The page on colleges that accept FAFSA explains the Pell Grant and what it covers.` },
  { q: 'Why does my school’s price differ from the median?', a: 'A median is the middle of all colleges of one type: half charge more, half charge less. Your school’s price also depends on your residence, your level, your program and the year, since most colleges raise tuition a little each year. Pick “I know my school’s price” in the calculator to use the real figure.' },
  { q: 'Do I need to give my e-mail or phone number?', a: 'No. There is no form and no account. Many sites about online degrees ask for your contact details and pass them to colleges that pay for them; this one does not. The calculators run in your browser and nothing you type leaves it.' },
];
const resume = `An online degree is priced by the credit. Colleges that take federal student aid report their per-credit charge to IPEDS, the national survey of colleges run by the National Center for Education Statistics, and for ${P.ipeds.chargesYear} the median public university charged its own residents ${m(N.medPub4)} a credit, against ${m(N.medPub4out)} for students from other states, ${m(N.medNpf4)} at private nonprofit universities and ${m(N.medFp4)} at for-profit ones. A bachelor’s degree asks for about 120 credits, an associate degree about 60 and a master’s 30 to 36, so the price per credit decides most of the bill. Required fees add a few hundred to a few thousand dollars a year, transfer credits come off the total, and grants such as the Pell Grant reduce what you pay. ${num(N.exc)} students, ${pc(shareOnline, 0)} of all college students, took every course at a distance in ${P.ipeds.enrollmentTerm}.`;
const jsonLd = [webApplicationSchema({ name: 'Online degree cost calculator', description: 'Tuition and fees of an online associate, bachelor’s or master’s degree from the per-credit charges US colleges report to IPEDS, with transfer credits, pace and grants.', path: route('home', lang), lang }), websiteSchema(lang), organizationSchema(lang), faqSchema(faqs, lang)];
const groups = GROUPS_ORDER.map((g) => ({ g, pages: PAGES.filter((p) => p.group === g) })).filter((x) => x.pages.length);
const title = `Online Degree Cost 2026: Price per Credit at ${num(N.n)} Colleges`;
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={`Online degree cost from the price per credit ${num(N.n)} US colleges report to the federal IPEDS file: your degree, your state, your transfer credits and grants.`} lang={lang} jsonLd={jsonLd} faqs={faqs.map((f, i) => ({ ...f, open: i < 2 }))}>
  <header class="mb-3">
    <h1 class="text-[1.75rem] font-bold text-navy-900 max-sm:leading-tight sm:text-3xl">Online degree cost: your degree, credit by credit, at the price colleges report</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-base text-navy-700 sm:text-lg">Online degree cost is the price per credit times the credits you still need. Public universities charge state residents a median of {m(N.medPub4)} a credit, about {m(N.medPub4 * 120)} for a 120-credit bachelor’s before fees. Pick your degree and type of school below; nothing you type is sent.</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-navy-600">Independent site: not a college, not a lead seller, not affiliated with the U.S. Department of Education or any school named.</p>
  </header>
  <div data-outil class="mb-4"><Tool tool={{ kind: 'mini:degree-cost' }} primary={true} /></div>
  <Clamp text={resume} id="clamp-home" lang={lang} class="mb-3" />
  <p class="mb-6 text-sm text-navy-600">Federal files read on {h.read} · {AUTHOR_NAME} · <a href={route('method', lang)} class="underline hover:text-navy-900">How the figures are built</a> · <a href={route('about', lang)} class="underline hover:text-navy-900">Who publishes this site</a></p>

  <section class="not-prose my-8" aria-labelledby="figures-title">
    <h2 id="figures-title" class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy-900">Four numbers from the federal files</h2>
    <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {figures.map((f) => <div class="border-l-4 border-accent-600 bg-white pl-4"><dt class="text-2xl font-bold tabular-nums text-navy-900">{f.fig}</dt><dd class="text-navy-700">{f.what}</dd></div>)}
    </dl>
  </section>

  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2>Online degree cost by type of school</h2>
    <p>The type of college sets the order of magnitude before anything else. The table gives the median per-credit charge of each type, its median yearly fees, and what that comes to for a 120-credit bachelor’s with four years of fees. It is a sticker price: grants and transfer credits come off it, and room and board are not part of an online student’s bill.</p>
    <Fragment set:html={h.table(['Type of university', 'Per credit', 'Fees per year', '120 credits, 4 years of fees'], types, `Medians of the ${P.ipeds.chargesYear} charges reported to IPEDS by four-year colleges. Undergraduate per-credit charge for part-time students; required fees for full-time students.`, ['l', 'r', 'r', 'r'])} />
    <p>Community colleges are cheaper still: a median of {m(N.medPub2d)} a credit for residents of their district and {m(N.medPub2)} for the rest of the state, which is why many students take their first 60 credits there. The <a href={route('online-associates-degree', lang)}>associate degree page</a> shows what that route saves on a bachelor’s.</p>

    <h2>Five steps to a budget you can trust</h2>
    <p><strong>1. Count the credits you still need.</strong> The program sheet gives the total; your transcript gives what you have. Credits from an earlier college, from exams such as CLEP or from military training may count, and every accepted credit is one you do not pay for again.</p>
    <p><strong>2. Find the price for your residence.</strong> On the school’s tuition page, look for the online rate per credit hour, for residents and for non-residents. A public university is often a bargain for its own state and an expensive choice for everyone else.</p>
    <p><strong>3. Add the fees.</strong> Technology, distance learning, registration and graduation fees are charged per term or per year. Spread over a year of credits they can add tens of dollars to every credit.</p>
    <p><strong>4. Choose a pace.</strong> Two courses a term, three terms a year, means 18 credits a year and close to seven years for a bachelor’s. A faster pace costs more each month but the same in tuition, and fewer years of fees.</p>
    <p><strong>5. Take off grants, not loans.</strong> A Pell Grant, a state grant or employer tuition help lowers the price; a loan only spreads it out. The calculator’s “grants per year” line is for money you never repay.</p>

    <h2>The twelve largest online schools, side by side</h2>
    <p>Twelve institutions teach close to a million fully online students between them. The table gives what each one reports: its price per credit for residents, or its flat yearly price, and the share of entering students who earned a degree or certificate within eight years.</p>
    <Fragment set:html={h.table(['School', 'Fully online students', 'Price reported', '120 credits with fees', 'Award in 8 years'], largest, `Enrollment ${P.ipeds.enrollmentTerm} and charges ${P.ipeds.chargesYear} (IPEDS); award rate of students who entered in ${P.scorecard.awardCohort} (College Scorecard). Flat-rate schools: yearly price at 30 credits a year.`, ['l', 'r', 'r', 'r', 'r'])} />
    <p>Size says nothing about price or results: the table holds both the cheapest and some of the most expensive per-credit prices on this site. The <a href={route('largest-online-universities', lang)}>largest online universities page</a> compares any two of the twenty largest in a calculator.</p>

    <h2>Where online students live and study</h2>
    <p>Online students mostly enroll close to home, at their state’s public colleges, and in a few states with very large online universities. Ranked by the number of fully online students enrolled at the state’s colleges:</p>
    <ul>
      {topStates.map((s) => <li><strong>{s.name}</strong>: {num(s.exc)} fully online students at {num(s.nOnline)} colleges; median public price {s.medPub ? m(s.medPub) : 'not reported'} a credit for residents.</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>Utah and New Hampshire rank high only because Western Governors University and Southern New Hampshire University are based there; their students live everywhere. The <a href={route('online-colleges-by-state', lang)}>state page</a> covers all fifty states and the District of Columbia.</p>

    <h2>Finishing is part of the price</h2>
    <p>A degree you do not finish still costs the credits you paid for. College Scorecard follows every student who entered a college in {P.scorecard.awardCohort}, first-time or transfer, full-time or part-time, for eight years. At the median college where most students study online, {pc(N.medOm8Online, 0)} held a degree or certificate from it at the end; at colleges where most students are on campus, {pc(N.medOm8Campus, 0)}. Online students are older and more often work and study part time, which explains part of the gap. Still, two schools with the same price can lead to very different results, and the <a href={route('online-college-graduation-rates', lang)}>graduation rates page</a> lets you compare them.</p>

    <h2>What the federal files cannot tell you</h2>
    <p>The figures here are those colleges declared for {P.ipeds.chargesYear}; the files for the next year were not yet published when this site was built. Prices have usually risen a little since. IPEDS also has no field for a separate online rate, for program fees in nursing or engineering, or for the cost of books and proctored exams. Some colleges in the files have closed or merged since they were drawn. Read each figure as a solid starting point, then check the school’s own tuition page before you enroll.</p>
    <p>No figure on this site comes from a ranking, an advertiser or a school’s marketing. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists the files, the variables and every rule used to build the tables.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="mt-10 mb-8" aria-labelledby="guides-title">
    <h2 id="guides-title" class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy-900">Every page, by question</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
      {groups.map(({ g, pages }) => (
        <div class="border-t-4 border-accent-600 pt-3">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-navy-900">{groupLabel(g)}</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2">{pages.map((p) => <li><a href={route(p.id, lang)} class="font-medium text-accent-700 underline-offset-2 hover:underline">{p.nav}</a></li>)}</ul>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <Sources lang={lang} intro="Each price, enrollment count and outcome on this page comes from the federal files below, published by the U.S. Department of Education and downloaded on" />
  </div>
</PageLayout>
